  "problems": [
    "story: the dates blur two facts. Portugal used azulejos from the late 15th century, but those early tiles were mostly imported from Seville. Portuguese workshops, mainly in Lisbon, began painting tin-glazed tiles in the 16th century. As written, Portuguese tile makers are credited with 15th-century work.",
    "story: it says what azulejos are and how they map to a screen, but not what they stand for. Rule 1 (meaning before motif) needs their place in Portuguese life and the documented origin of the name in the Arabic al-zulayj, which also matters to Gulf readers.",
    "credit: repeats the inaccurate claim that Portuguese makers glazed and painted these tiles from the 15th century.",
    "rhythm: a border band one quarter of a tile wide is not documented azulejo practice. Borders and friezes on panels are usually laid as full tiles or half tiles, so the grammar should follow that."
  ],
  "fix": {
    "story": "Azulejos are painted, tin-glazed ceramic tiles made in Portugal. They reached Portugal from Seville in the late 15th century, and from the 16th century Portuguese workshops, mainly in Lisbon, made and painted their own. Since then tile makers and painters have covered facades, churches, palaces and railway stations with them, and today azulejos are one of the clearest signs of Portuguese identity, best known in cobalt blue on white. The word itself comes from the Arabic al-zulayj, a small polished stone. Each tile is a small square, but laid together the tiles build one large pattern, and border tiles frame the whole panel. That logic of a small module adding up to a big field suits a screen, where cards, cells and grids already repeat in squares.",
    "credit": "This lens draws on the Portuguese azulejo tradition and credits the tile makers and painters of Portugal, whose workshops have glazed and painted these tiles from the 16th century to today.",
    "rhythm": "A strict square repeat where the real pattern only appears at the scale of four or more tiles, framed by a border band laid as one row of full tiles or half tiles."
  }
}
```

