 "problems": [
  "story: says UNESCO 'runs a safeguarding programme'. UNESCO does not run one. It inscribed 'Al Sadu, traditional weaving skills in the United Arab Emirates' on its List of Intangible Cultural Heritage in Need of Urgent Safeguarding in 2011. Safeguarding is carried out by the state and the communities.",
  "story: meaning before motif is thin. It names no materials and no uses, so the reader does not learn what the weaving is for. Added the documented fibres (sheep wool, camel hair, goat hair) and uses (tent dividers, rugs, cushions, camel and horse trappings).",
  "credit: repeats the wrong UNESCO framing. It should name the actual inscription and year. It should also place the weavers more exactly than 'their communities' alone."
 ],
 "fix": {
  "story": "Al Sadu is the weaving of Bedouin women of the Arabian Peninsula, made on a ground loom from sheep wool, camel hair and goat hair. Its long bands and small geometric figures repeat in strict order. Families wove it for tent dividers, rugs, cushions and the trappings of camels and horses, and the craft carries skill and knowledge passed from older weavers to younger ones. In 2011 UNESCO inscribed Al Sadu, traditional weaving skills in the United Arab Emirates, on its List of Intangible Cultural Heritage in Need of Urgent Safeguarding, because fewer young people now learn it. On a screen its grammar maps cleanly: bands become section dividers and progress tracks, small figures become step markers and bullets, and the slight unevenness of the hand keeps the layout human.",
  "credit": "Al Sadu is the craft of Bedouin women of the Arabian Peninsula, kept alive and handed down by their families and communities. Al Sadu, traditional weaving skills in the United Arab Emirates, has been on UNESCO's List of Intangible Cultural Heritage in Need of Urgent Safeguarding since 2011."
 }
}
