 "problems": [
  "story: says the planks are 'joined with rope lashing'. Gulf dhows of recent centuries were fastened with nails. Sewn-plank boats are an older and narrower tradition, so this claim cannot stand as written.",
  "story: says the planks are 'laid over ribs'. That describes frame-first building, which I cannot confirm for Gulf dhows. The safe claim is that shipwrights shape long curved planks and fit ribs in the hull at a steady spacing.",
  "story: implies every dhow has carved stern panels. Carved sterns belong to some larger types such as the baghlah and the ghanjah, so the claim is narrowed to them.",
  "story: rope belongs to the rigging, not the hull joins, so the rope reference now sits with the sail. This keeps the crossed binds in shapeGrammar and the 'lash in' motion grounded in fact."
 ],
 "fix": {
  "story": "Dhows are the wooden sailing vessels of the Gulf and the western Indian Ocean, built by hand by shipwrights in ports such as Kuwait, Bahrain, Sur in Oman and Dubai. Sailing communities used them for pearling, fishing and trade. Shipwrights shape the hull from long curved planks and fit ribs inside it at a steady spacing. A triangular lateen sail, rigged with rope, drives the vessel, and larger dhows such as the baghlah and the ghanjah carry carved stern panels. On a screen the ribs give a firm grid, and the long plank curves soften it, so layouts feel built by hand and still read clearly."
 }
